WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.310
A vaccine is a type of treatment for a
disease which alerts the immune system

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.200
and causes the immune system to start to act against the disease.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.700
 A drug, on the other hand, is a molecule of some type which is able to 

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.400
treat the symptoms of a disease.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:21.400
Our goal is to develop interventions that can help save and protect the Tasmanian devils in the wild.

00:00:21.439 --> 00:00:25.109
We're trying to develop a vaccine which
can help to prevent

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:27.800
those devils in the wild from getting
the disease.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.700
Secondly, we're interested in developing a drug which can help 

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.800
cure those devils that already have signs of the disease and also 

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.700
prevent them from spreading it further to other devils.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:43.400
So I think a combination of these two approaches is really essential for saving the Tasmanian devils in the wild.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.000
The really good thing about our immune system

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.700
is that it remembers particular pathogens.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.000
So the way that we hope to make a vaccine against Devil Facial Tumour Disease

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.200
is to take these tumour cells in the lab and to make them express

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.800
very particular molecules or proteins on the surface of their cells

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:08.900
And then we would treat these DFTD cells in some way to make them non-harmful to the devils

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.200
so basically, stop them growing.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:16.700
That means that when we put them back into the devils they won't divide and they won't cause tumours. 

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.200
But what they will do is they will allow the immune system to have a look at them

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.700
and remember them for next time.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:30.600
So then, the next time that the Tasmanian devils see these tumour cells they'll be able to mount an immune response against them

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.200
because the immune system will be, what we call, primed to see these DFTD cells.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.600
At the moment we're working with the
genome sequence

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:42.030
and with a large drug screen here at the Sanger Institute

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:46.960
and we've used hundreds of candidate
drugs and screened their effectiveness in

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.300
killing or preventing proliferation of the Tasmanian Devil cancer cells 

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.300
and we have found a couple of very interesting candidates

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.090
that seem to be quite effective against the devil cancer

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.600
and we're now using these drugs in Tasmania

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.600
to see if they're effective in Tasmanian devils against the cancer.

